{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 requires implementing a `solve(case)` function that performs grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree.\n\nKey rules from the problem:\n1. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\n2. Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes (process deeper nodes first)\n3. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\n4. Use node ID as the stable tie-break at equal depth\n5. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order\n\nThe output schema requires:\n- `removed_node_ids`: array of strings (in removal order)\n- `retained_node_ids`: array of strings (sorted)\n\nLet me think through the algorithm:\n\n1. Build a tree from the nodes\n2. Process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deepest first), with node ID as tie-break at equal depth\n3. For each node (in that order):\n   - Skip if not removable\n   - Skip if it's a required symbol\n   - Skip if removing it would leave some required symbol with no representation\n   - Skip if it has retained children (can't remove a node with retained children)\n   - If we can remove it (tentatively), mark it as removed\n4. Return retained and removed nodes\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nSo a node can be removed only if:\n- It's removable\n- It doesn't have any retained children\n- Removing it doesn't eliminate all instances of any required symbol\n\nWait, \"a required symbol\" - does this mean we can't remove a node whose symbol is a required symbol? Or that we can't remove if doing so would leave required symbols unrepresented?\n\nThe rule says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" - I think \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So nodes with symbols in required_symbols cannot be removed.\n\nBut then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this means we check if after removal, every required symbol still has at least one node representing it in the retained set.\n\nLet me re-read more carefully:\n\n- \"Never remove the root\" - the root node (id=\"root\") can never be removed\n- \"a required symbol\" - a node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed  \n- \"a node with retained children\" - if a node has children that are still retained, it cannot be removed\n- \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - after removal, check that each required symbol still has at least one retained node\n\nSo the algorithm is:\n\n1. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (for stable tie-break)\n2. Maintain a set of retained nodes (initially all nodes)\n3. For each node in that order:\n   - If not removable: skip\n   - If symbol is in required_symbols: skip (but wait, root symbol \"root\" is in required_symbols - so root is protected by both rules)\n   - If node has retained children: skip\n   - Tentatively: check if removing this node would still leave all required symbols represented in the remaining retained set\n   - If yes: remove it (add to removed list)\n4. Return removed_node_ids (in removal order), retained_node_ids (sorted)\n\nWait, but the ordering for processing - \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\". So we process nodes in order of depth descending, then node ID ascending.\n\nLet me trace through the first example:\nNodes:\n- root: depth 0, removable=false, symbol=\"root\"\n- n1: depth 1, removable=false, symbol=\"s1\"\n- n2: depth 1, removable=true, symbol=\"s2\"\n- n3: depth 1, removable=false, symbol=\"s0\"\n- n4: depth 2, removable=true, symbol=\"s1\", parent=n2\n- n5: depth 2, removable=false, symbol=\"s2\", parent=n3\n- n6: depth 2, removable=true, symbol=\"s0\", parent=n1\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order (depth desc, id asc for ties):\n- Depth 2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by id: n4, n5, n6)\n- Depth 1: n1, n2, n3 (sorted by id: n1, n2, n3)\n- Depth 0: root\n\nProcessing n4 (depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s1\"):\n- symbol \"s1\" is in required_symbols, so skip? Wait, that would mean n4 can't be removed because its symbol is s1.\n- But n1 also has symbol s1 and n1 is retained, so s1 would still be represented.\n\nHmm, I need to reconsider the rule \"Never remove... a required symbol\". Does this mean:\n(a) Never remove a node whose symbol is a required symbol, OR\n(b) Never remove a node if doing so would leave a required symbol unrepresented\n\nThe \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" seems to be rule (b). So rule \"Never remove a required symbol\" might be rule (a) - never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols regardless.\n\nBut that seems redundant with rule (b) if the required symbol has multiple instances. Let me think again...\n\nActually, I think \"required symbol\" in \"never remove a required symbol\" refers to nodes that ARE required_symbols - meaning nodes whose symbols are in required_symbols. This is separate from \"tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nBut wait - if we never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, and the required_symbols are [\"root\", \"s1\"], then n1 (s1), n4 (s1) would never be removable because of their symbol. But n1 is also not removable=true...\n\nActually n4 is removable=true but has symbol s1 (required). Under interpretation (a), n4 would never be removed. But n1 already has symbol s1 and is non-removable, so s1 is always represented anyway.\n\nLet me try both interpretations and see what makes sense:\n\nInterpretation A: Skip if node.symbol in required_symbols (in addition to other checks)\nInterpretation B: Only check that removing the node leaves all required symbols still represented\n\nUnder interpretation B (only the \"represented\" check):\n- Processing n4: symbol=s1, s1 also represented by n1. So removing n4: remaining s1 instances = n1. All required symbols still represented. n4 has no children, so no retained children issue. Removable=true. → REMOVE n4\n- Processing n5: removable=false → SKIP\n- Processing n6: removable=true, symbol=s0 (not required), no children. After removal: s0 represented by n3. s1 represented by n1. → REMOVE n6\n- Processing n1: removable=false → SKIP\n- Processing n2: removable=true, symbol=s2, but n2's parent... wait, n4 was a child of n2. Since n4 was removed, n2 has no retained children. After removal: s2 represented by n5. s1 represented by n1. → REMOVE n2\n- Processing n3: removable=false → SKIP\n- Processing root: removable=false → SKIP\n\nSo removed: [n4, n6, n2] (in that order)\nRetained: [root, n1, n3, n5] sorted\n\nUnder interpretation A:\n- Processing n4: symbol=s1 ∈ required_symbols → SKIP\n- Processing n5: removable=false → SKIP\n- Processing n6: removable=true, symbol=s0, no children. After removal: required symbols still represented (root: root, s1: n1 and n4). → REMOVE n6\n- Processing n1: removable=false → SKIP\n- Processing n2: removable=true, symbol=s2, children: n4 (retained). Has retained children → SKIP\n- Processing n3: removable=false → SKIP\n- Processing root: removable=false → SKIP\n\nSo removed: [n6]\nRetained: [root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5] sorted\n\nHmm, interpretation B seems more aggressive and makes more sense for a \"reduction\" algorithm. The goal is to reduce the tree as much as possible.\n\nBut then \"Never remove a required symbol\" might mean never remove the last remaining node that represents a required symbol - which is captured by \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nActually, I think the three rules are:\n1. Never remove the root node itself\n2. Never remove a node that has retained children (structural constraint)\n3. Only remove a node if after removal, all required symbols still have at least one representative\n\nAnd \"required symbol\" in rule 1 might actually be interpreted as: \"Never remove [the root, [a node whose symbol is] a required symbol [that is the last instance], or a node with retained children]\" - essentially the \"tentatively remove only if...\" rule covers this.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" - I think this means three separate categories:\n1. The root node\n2. Any node whose symbol is a required symbol  \n3. Any node with retained children\n\nAnd \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is an additional check.\n\nBut if we never remove nodes with required symbols, then the \"tentatively\" check is just extra insurance/clarification.\n\nWait, but actually if we can never remove nodes with required symbols, and required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\", then nodes n1 and n4 can never be removed (they have symbol s1). This is very restrictive.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The task says \"grammar-guided node reduction\" using \"Perses syntax-guided reduction.\" In Perses, the goal is to remove as many nodes as possible while preserving \"interestingness.\" The rules say:\n\n- Nodes that are \"required\" (their symbol is required) cannot be removed\n- The root cannot be removed\n- A node with retained children cannot be removed (you'd need to remove children first)\n- A node can only be removed if all required symbols remain covered\n\nUnder interpretation A (never remove nodes with required symbols):\nThis makes sense because in a grammar, certain non-terminals are \"required\" - you must have at least one parse of each required non-terminal. But actually in Perses, the \"required symbols\" are the symbols that must be present in the reduced tree for it to remain interesting/valid.\n\nI'll go with interpretation A (more conservative and explicitly stated) for the \"never remove a required symbol\" rule, plus check that all required symbols remain represented.\n\nActually wait - if we never remove nodes with required symbols (s1, root), then the \"tentatively check\" is mostly about nodes whose symbols aren't required but whose removal would somehow affect required symbols' representation. But if we already protect nodes with required symbols, removing other nodes can't eliminate required symbols from the tree...\n\nUnless: a node with a non-required symbol could be the parent of required-symbol nodes? If we remove a parent, we implicitly remove its children too? But the rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" prevents this. So if we can't remove a node with retained children, removing a parent would require its children to already be removed.\n\nI think the checks are:\n1. Skip if removable=false\n2. Skip if node is root\n3. Skip if node.symbol in required_symbols (it's a required symbol itself)\n4. Skip if node has retained children\n5. Tentatively remove: check if all required symbols still represented after removal\n\nWith these checks:\n- Check 3 is basically saying: a node whose symbol is required cannot be removed even if removable=true\n- Check 5 is: for nodes whose symbols are NOT required, verify that removing them doesn't somehow break required symbol coverage (this would be rare given check 3, but may be a safety net or handle edge cases)\n\nActually, I realize: if we never remove nodes with required symbols (check 3), and we never remove nodes with retained children (check 4), then the only way a required symbol could become unrepresented is if somehow... well, it can't, because we're keeping all nodes with required symbols.\n\nUnless a required symbol has no nodes at all? No, that can't happen because we start with all nodes retained.\n\nSo check 5 becomes redundant with checks 3+4. But it's listed as a separate rule, so maybe interpretation B is correct and \"never remove a required symbol\" is the same as check 5.\n\nOK I think I'm overanalyzing this. Let me go with the interpretation that makes the algorithm consistent:\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\" = \n- Never remove the root node (id=\"root\" or parent=null)\n- Never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols \n- Never remove a node that still has children in the retained set\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" = additional check\n\nActually, you know what, let me just implement it with all checks and see:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build index\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Sort processing order: depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if root (parent is None)\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if symbol is required\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if has retained children\n        has_retained_children = any(c in retained for c in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove: check all required symbols still represented\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_in_retained = set(node_map[i][\"symbol\"] for i in test_retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_retained):\n            continue\n        \n        # Remove it\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)\n    }\n```\n\nLet me trace through example 1 with this:\n\nNodes: root(s=root,d=0,rem=F), n1(s=s1,d=1,rem=F), n2(s=s2,d=1,rem=T), n3(s=s0,d=1,rem=F), n4(s=s1,d=2,rem=T,p=n2), n5(s=s2,d=2,rem=F,p=n3), n6(s=s0,d=2,rem=T,p=n1)\nrequired_symbols: {\"root\", \"s1\"}\n\nProcessing order (depth desc, id asc): n4, n5, n6 (depth 2), n1, n2, n3 (depth 1), root (depth 0)\n\nn4: removable=T, parent=n2 (not None), symbol=s1 ∈ required_symbols → SKIP\nn5: removable=F → SKIP\nn6: removable=T, parent=n1 (not None), symbol=s0 ∉ required_symbols, children of n6: none → no retained children. Tentative: retained - {n6} still has root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2). required_symbols = {root, s1}: root ✓, s1 ✓. → REMOVE n6. retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}\n\nn1: removable=F → SKIP\nn2: removable=T, parent=root, symbol=s2 ∉ required_symbols. Children of n2: [n4]. n4 ∈ retained → has retained children → SKIP\nn3: removable=F → SKIP\nroot: parent=None → SKIP\n\nResult: removed=[n6], retained=[n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root] sorted = [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]\n\nHmm, that only removes n6. Is that right? The reduction is quite minimal because n2 can't be removed since n4 (retained) is its child, and n4 can't be removed because s1 is required.\n\nLet me double-check: n4 has symbol s1, which is in required_symbols. So under my implementation, n4 cannot be removed (rule: skip if symbol in required_symbols). Then n2 has n4 as a retained child, so n2 cannot be removed.\n\nThat makes logical sense: we need at least one s1 node (n4 or n1), and n1 is not removable, so we must keep n1. n4 is also an s1 and is removable, but since s1 is required, we keep n4. But then n2 (n4's parent) has a retained child and can't be removed.\n\nWait, but if n1 (non-removable) already represents s1, do we really need to keep n4? That's what interpretation B would handle - check if s1 is still represented after removal.\n\nUnder interpretation B (only check #5, no check #3):\nn4: removable=T, parent=n2 (not None), no children. Tentative: retained - {n4} = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6}. Symbols: root, s1(n1), s2(n2,n5), s0(n3,n6). required_symbols ⊆ {root, s1, s2, s0} ✓. → REMOVE n4!\n\nThen:\nn5: removable=F → SKIP\nn6: removable=T, no children. Tentative: retained - {n6} = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}. Symbols: root, s1(n1), s2(n2,n5), s0(n3). ✓ → REMOVE n6!\n\nn1: removable=F → SKIP\nn2: removable=T, parent=root, symbol=s2. n4 removed, so children of n2 in retained: none. Tentative: retained - {n2} = {root, n1, n3, n5}. Symbols: root, s1(n1), s2(n5), s0(n3). ✓ → REMOVE n2!\n\nn3: removable=F → SKIP\nroot: parent=None → SKIP\n\nResult: removed=[n4, n6, n2] (in that order), retained=[n1, n3, n5, root] sorted\n\nThis feels more like a reduction algorithm - removes more nodes. Interpretation B seems more appropriate for \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" which aims to minimize the tree.\n\nBut \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\" - if \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is a required symbol, then we'd use interpretation A (keeping n4).\n\nHmm. Let me re-read the constraints:\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nI think these are meant to be read together. The \"tentatively\" check ensures required symbols remain represented, which is THE constraint about required symbols. The \"never remove a required symbol\" might be redundant phrasing or might specifically mean: \"never remove the last node representing a required symbol\" - but that's exactly what the tentative check ensures.\n\nSo the hard constraints are:\n1. removable=true\n2. Not the root (parent=None or id=\"root\")\n3. No retained children\n4. After removal, all required symbols still represented\n\nUnder this interpretation B, my trace above gives: removed=[n4, n6, n2], retained=[n1, n3, n5, root].\n\nI'll go with interpretation B as it's the more complete/natural reduction algorithm and the \"tentatively\" check makes \"never remove a required symbol\" redundant (it's subsumed).\n\nActually, wait. I just re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This is stated as three hard rules. Then separately \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is an additional check. These seem to be distinct constraints:\n\nRule 1: Never remove root\nRule 2: Never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols  \nRule 3: Never remove a node with retained children\nRule 4: Only remove if all required symbols remain represented after removal\n\nIf rules 2 and 4 are both present, rule 2 is a stronger constraint on nodes with required symbols, and rule 4 is a general coverage check for all required symbols.\n\nBut under interpretation A with all 4 rules:\n- n4 (symbol=s1, required) → can never be removed → n2 (parent of n4) can never be removed\n\nThis leads to a less aggressive reduction. \n\nUnder interpretation B with rules 1, 3, 4 (no rule 2):\n- n4 can be removed if s1 is still covered by n1\n- n2 can then be removed since n4 is gone\n\nI think the problem statement's phrasing \"Never remove the root, a required symbol\" might mean: never remove a node if that node's symbol is a required symbol AND removing it would leave that symbol unrepresented. That's essentially rule 4 for the specific case of the node itself.\n\nYou know what, I'll just go with interpretation B (no rule 2 - the required symbol check is only about coverage/representation). This produces the more aggressive reduction which is the goal of Perses-style reduction. The \"required symbols\" constraint is entirely captured by \"all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nLet me code the final implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build node map and children map\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Process order: depth descending, then id ascending (stable tie-break)\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Must be removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove the root\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove a node with retained children\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove: check all required symbols still represented\n        candidate_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_covered = set(node_map[i][\"symbol\"] for i in candidate_retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_covered):\n            continue\n        \n        # Perform removal\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)\n    }\n```\n\nLet me verify with example 1:\n- Processing order: n4(d=2), n5(d=2), n6(d=2), n1(d=1), n2(d=1), n3(d=1), root(d=0)\n- n4: removable=T, parent=n2 (not None), children=[none], tentative: {root,n1,n2,n3,n5,n6} covers {root,s1(n1),s2(n2,n5),s0(n3,n6)} → s1✓ root✓ → REMOVE n4\n- n5: removable=F → skip\n- n6: removable=T, parent=n1, children=[], tentative: {root,n1,n2,n3,n5} covers {root,s1(n1),s2(n2,n5),s0(n3)} → root✓ s1✓ → REMOVE n6\n- n1: removable=F → skip\n- n2: removable=T, parent=root, children=[n4](removed, not in retained), retained children: none. Tentative: {root,n1,n3,n5} covers {root,s1(n1),s0(n3),s2(n5)} → root✓ s1✓ → REMOVE n2\n- n3: removable=F → skip\n- root: parent=None → skip\n\nResult: removed=[n4,n6,n2], retained=[n1,n3,n5,root] → sorted: [n1,n3,n5,root]\n\nThis looks like good reduction. All examples have the same structure (same required_symbols, same topology), just different utilities.\n\nThe utility field doesn't seem to affect the algorithm based on the description (it's about grammar-guided reduction, not utility-based).\n\nWait, let me double-check if the sorting of \"retained_node_ids\" should be lexicographic string sort. \"n1\" \u003c \"n2\" \u003c ... \u003c \"n5\" \u003c \"root\" (since \"r\" \u003e \"n\" in ASCII... wait no, \"n\" = 110, \"r\" = 114, so \"root\" \u003e \"n*\" alphabetically). \n\nActually lexicographic: \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\" → n comes before r, so: n1, n3, n5, root. That's correct.\n\nLet me also verify example 2 (same structure, different utilities):\nSame topology, same required_symbols → same result: removed=[n4,n6,n2], retained=[n1,n3,n5,root]\n\nExample 3: Same. removed=[n4,n6,n2], retained=[n1,n3,n5,root]\n\nThe implementation looks correct. Let me write the final JSON response.\n\nThe implementation needs to be a valid Python string with proper JSON escaping. Let me write it out:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        candidate_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_covered = set(node_map[i][\"symbol\"] for i in candidate_retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_covered):\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)\n    }\n```\n\nNow I need to format this as JSON with proper escaping.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None:\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    # Process deepest nodes first; use node id as stable tie-break at equal depth\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n    removed = []\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Must be flagged removable\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Never remove the root\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Never remove a node that still has retained children\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Tentatively remove: verify every required symbol remains represented\\n        candidate_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_covered = set(node_map[i][\\\"symbol\\\"] for i in candidate_retained)\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_covered):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Commit the removal\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": sorted(retained)\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01x7rG0TdatasS0NT4tqoQEq","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2428},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2428,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":34,"output_tokens":7161,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6736},"service_tier":"standard"}}